<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hungarian Revolution of 1848</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1849">1849</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Hungarian Revolution</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hungarian Revolution of 1848 was an uprising seeking Hungarian autonomy and then independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule, part of the wider <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1848">revolutions of 1848</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. It was crushed in 1849 with the help of Russian troops sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> Nicholas I. The intervention illustrates the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>'s role as a guardian of conservative order in Europe.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hungarian-revolution-of-1848.jpg" alt="Hungarian Revolution of 1848" width="290" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mihály Zichy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:March15.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Unlike other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> had an old constitution that limited the Crown, going back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1222">Golden Bull of 1222</a>, and a separate parliament, the Diet. Under Metternich's policy of stability the Diet was not summoned after 1811, but writers such as Mihály Vörösmarty and Ferenc Kölcsey kept alive the ideal of political liberty. The crisis came from abroad: on 3 March 1848, after news of the revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lajos-kossuth">Lajos Kossuth</a> demanded parliamentary government for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, and his speech was read aloud in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> before Metternich was overthrown on 13 March.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 17 March 1848 the emperor assented, and Lajos Batthyány formed the first Hungarian responsible government. The Twelve Points, called the March Laws, received royal assent on 10 April. The April laws created a democratic political system: Act V of 1848 turned the feudal Diet into an elected parliament with the widest right to vote in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> at the time and erased the privileges of the nobility. The government also demanded that taxes raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> be spent there and that parliament control the Hungarian regiments.</p><p>Conflict followed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a>, whom the Austrian court manipulated with conflicting promises, took up arms against the Hungarian government, and brutal incidents occurred. When Batthyány resigned, Kossuth became President of the Committee of National Defence at the end of September. In December Austrian forces under Field Marshal Windisch-Grätz entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, and the government left Pest for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debrecen">Debrecen</a>. Hungarian armies led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artur-gorgei">Artúr Görgei</a> won victories in the spring of 1849, and General Bem drove the Austrian and Russian troops out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> then sought help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Franz Joseph I kissed the hand of Nicholas I in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> on 21 May 1849, and Nicholas sent about 200,000 men under Field Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-paskevich">Ivan Paskevich</a>, while Julius Jacob von Haynau commanded the Austrian army. The Hungarian army of about 160,000 faced some 380,000 troops, and it was defeated.</p><p>Haynau was made plenipotentiary and ordered the execution of the 13 Martyrs of Arad, and Batthyány was executed on the same day in Pest. Hungary was placed under martial law, followed by passive resistance and, from 1851, the regency of Archduke Albrecht, who pursued a policy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanisation">Germanisation</a>. Many Hungarians later served in the Union Army in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>. The revolution is central to modern Hungarian national identity, and 15 March, the anniversary of its outbreak, is a national holiday.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hungarian Revolution of 1848” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…(1814–1815), where Alexander I took a leading part, Russia was confirmed as one of the great powers of the Concert of Europe, a status it maintained through active intervention in European affairs, including the suppression of the <b>Hungarian Revolution</b> in 1849 and repeated wars with the Ottoman Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
